<edge source="E:Risk:ai_bias" target="E:Concept:public_trust" id="0">
  <data key="d4">AFFECTS</data>
  <data key="d5">KDJ-AI-2026E1-P04-C31-S01-9970B5DC</data>
  <data key="d6">source</data>
  <data key="d7">NIST-SP-1270::P0004::SECTOWARDSASTANDARDFORIDENTIFYI::B0024</data>
  <data key="d8">These harmful outcomes, even if inadvertent, create significant challenges for cultivating public trust in artificial intelligence</data>
</edge>
<edge source="E:Risk:ai_bias" target="E:EvaluationMethod:tevv" id="0">
  <data key="d4">MEASURED_BY</data>
  <data key="d5">KDJ-AI-2026E1-P04-C31-S01-9970B5DC</data>
  <data key="d6">source</data>
  <data key="d7">NIST-SP-1270::P0005::SECTOWARDSASTANDARDFORIDENTIFYI::B0033</data>
  <data key="d8">describes three broad challenges for mitigating bias — datasets, testing and evaluation, and human factors</data>
</edge>
<edge source="E:Risk:ai_bias" target="E:Process:ai_lifecycle" id="0">
  <data key="d4">AFFECTS</data>
  <data key="d5">KDJ-AI-2026E1-P04-C31-S04-04090909</data>
  <data key="d6">source</data>
  <data key="d7">NIST-SP-1270::P0056::SEC4::B0321</data>
  <data key="d8">new requirements, many of which are contextual in nature, to the processes that comprise the AI lifecycle</data>
</edge>
<edge source="E:Risk:ai_bias" target="E:Publication:nist_paper_3_legal" id="0">
